==================================================
Assertions
==================================================
  Assertion checks:
    A1: All nibbles balanced at every round (bijective SPN) ✓
    A2: Single-nibble variation at R=2,6,10,14 ✓
    A3: Triple-nibble variation at R=1,5,9,13 ✓
    A4: Pairs maintain high diversity through R=3 ✓
    A5: Period-4 cycle confirmed ✓

==================================================
Summary
==================================================

  Key findings:

  • QUARTET preserves XOR-balance in all nibbles through
    all 16 rounds (trivially, because S-box is bijective).

  • However, structural entropy CONCENTRATES predictably:
    after every even round the variation shrinks to ONE
    nibble. After R=2, only W2 carries information.

  • Distinguisher: collect 16 encryptions of any
    single-nibble balanced set. After 2 rounds, 12 of
    16 ciphertext bits will be identical. Probability
    under random permutation ≈ 2⁻¹².

  • The cycle repeats with period 4. After R=4, the
    structure returns to the R=0 pattern (different
    concrete values, same abstract form).

  • Implication for construction modes: QUARTET can be
    used as a building block only if the construction
    compensates for the R=2 collapse. Any mode relying
    on integral survival beyond 2 rounds is weakened.

  PASS
======================================================================
QUARTET — invariant subspace check
======================================================================

Phase 1: Structural invariant proofs
----------------------------------------
  FOUND [D]: {x,x,x,x} (diagonal (dim 4)) — All 16 keys preserve equality of all nibbles.
  FOUND [A1]: {x,y,x,y} (alternating (dim 8)) — Round maps W0↔W2 and W1↔W3 while preserving pattern.
  FOUND [A2↔A3]: {x,y,y,x} ↔ {x,x,y,y} (adjacent-cycle (dim 8 each)) — Cyclically invariant: A2 maps to A3 in one round, A3 maps back to A2. Period-2 cycle for all 16 keys.

Phase 2: Randomized search for additional subspaces
----------------------------------------
  Testing 4096 random masks …
  Done in 0.0s. Checked=4096, rejected_filter=4096, rejected_full=0, suggested=0.

  No additional invariant 1-dim subspaces found.
  Combined with Phase 1 proof coverage, this strongly suggests
  that the only non-trivial invariant subspaces are D, A1, A2, A3.

Phase 3: Security implications
----------------------------------------
  Subspace sizes relative to 16-bit state space (2^16 = 65536):
    D  = 2^4 / 2^16 = 2^(-12) ≈ 1/4096
    A1 = 2^8 / 2^16 = 2^(-8)  = 1/256
    A2 = 2^8 / 2^16 = 2^(-8)  = 1/256
    A3 = 2^8 / 2^16 = 2^(-8)  = 1/256
  An attacker choosing plaintexts from any of these subspaces
  can distinguish QUARTET from a random permutation with
  advantage ≤ 1/256. This is below practical exploitability
  thresholds, but provides a small theoretical distinguishing
  advantage proportional to the subspace density.

  Total runtime: 0.1s

PASS
